activity1='''<article class="activity" id="four-lines"><p class="eyebrow">01 / Writing &amp; revision · about 5–7 minutes</p><h2>Four lines. Two versions.</h2><p>Make a listener picture an invented scene. Write, speak quietly, or think it through.</p><div class="example"><span>Original teaching example · fictional scene</span><p class="verse">The last bus pulled away.<br>I stood under the shelter.<br>The rain was loud.<br>I waited for a ride.</p></div><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Start your own scene.</strong> Use four lines about an ordinary object or fictional moment. You can also work from the example.</li><li><strong>Keep the first version.</strong> On paper, in your own notes, or in memory.</li><li><strong>Change one thing.</strong> Replace a vague detail, move a pause, or cut a word that does not help.</li><li><strong>Compare.</strong> What changed in the picture, meaning or sound?</li></ol><details><summary>See one possible revision</summary><p class="verse">The last bus pulled away.<br>I folded my ticket twice.<br>Rain rattled the shelter roof.<br>I watched for familiar headlights.</p><p>This version adds visible and audible details.</p></details><div class="note"><strong>If you want feedback</strong><p>Ask a willing listener what they remember and where they lose the idea.</p></div><h3>One learning connection</h3><p>Bring two versions of a paragraph, presentation or verse to a teacher or tutor. Ask: “Which detail makes the idea clearer?”</p><a class="button" href="next-step.html#writing">Find a next step for writing</a></article>'''
activity2='''<article class="activity" id="show"><p class="eyebrow">02 / Music business &amp; arithmetic · about 5 minutes</p><h2>What is left from a $500 show?</h2><p><strong>Invented scenario.</strong> Two collaborators receive a $500 fee. Travel costs $75 and an engineer costs $125. They agree to keep $100 for a future project and split the rest equally.</p><ol class="steps"><li>Subtract the two expenses. How much cash remains?</li><li>Set aside the agreed reserve. How much is available to split?</li><li>Divide that amount between the two people.</li><li>What needs to be agreed before the show? What cost might be missing?</li></ol><details><summary>Compare with the worked example</summary><dl class="budget"><div><dt>Fee received</dt><dd>$500</dd></div><div><dt>Travel + engineer</dt><dd>−$200</dd></div><div><dt>Cash after those expenses</dt><dd>$300</dd></div><div><dt>Agreed project reserve</dt><dd>−$100</dd></div><div><dt>Available to distribute</dt><dd>$200</dd></div><div><dt>Each person's share</dt><dd>$100</dd></div></dl><p>The reserve is cash kept for later, not automatically another expense. This does not calculate taxable profit or final after-tax income. Ask who approves costs, when payment happens and what happens if a cost changes.</p></details><h3>One learning connection</h3><p>Ask a math teacher or tutor to check your explanation of the split. For a real paid opportunity, ask a trusted adult to help find qualified advice about terms, rights and payment.</p><a class="button" href="next-step.html#business">Find a next step for music business</a></article>'''
support='''<h1>What would help right now?</h1><div class="support-grid"><section id="writing"><h2>Get better at writing</h2><p class="ask">“Can you look at these two versions and tell me where the idea gets clearer?”</p><p>Try a teacher, tutor, librarian or a trusted person in an established arts program.</p></section><section id="credits"><h2>Catch up or check my credits</h2><p>Ask a school counselor or education support worker about one missing assignment, credit or enrollment question.</p><p class="ask">“What is one realistic step, and who can help me take it?”</p><p>If you are not enrolled, ask about locally approved options and eligibility.</p></section><section id="business"><h2>Understand music business</h2><p class="ask">“What will I actually receive, what work is expected, and who controls the recording?”</p><p>For real agreements, involve a trusted adult and qualified advice. Do not share a contract or personal information with strangers online.</p></section><section id="help"><h2>School is not working for me</h2><p>Safety, transport, learning support, work and missing credits need practical help.</p><p class="ask">“This is the part I need help with. What can we change first?”</p><p>Choose a reliable counselor, teacher, caregiver, youth advocate or education worker you already know or can meet through a trusted organization.</p></section><section id="money"><h2>I need money now</h2><p>Ask a trusted adult or youth-program staff member about age-appropriate work and immediate support, alongside education options.</p><p>This site cannot provide a job, case management or financial support.</p></section></div>'''
guide='''<h1>For adults</h1><p class="intro">A flexible 20–25 minute session for ages 13–18, individually or in a small group.</p><section><h2>Before the session</h2><p>Read a story, try an activity and check the source note. Bring paper and pencils, or open the offline packet. Offer reading aloud, silent reflection and spoken responses. Do not infer reading level from age. Let the participant select a skill without choosing an artist.</p><p>Explain your setting's actual confidentiality limits.</p></section><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Invite · 2 minutes.</strong> “Would you like to try something connected to writing or music?” Declining is fine.</li><li><strong>Choose · 3 minutes.</strong> Let them select a story or go directly to an activity. Ask what the artist actually practiced.</li><li><strong>Try · 5–7 minutes.</strong> Use a fictional scene or the invented budget. No public performance; keep creative work private.</li><li><strong>Reflect · 5 minutes.</strong> “What changed? What did not help?” Discuss the intended meaning, not whether their dialect is correct.</li><li><strong>Connect · 5 minutes.</strong> “Could a class, library, program or person help with this?” Accept that a connection may feel forced.</li><li><strong>Close · 2 minutes.</strong> Offer a voluntary next action and a reliable support route. Name who can help and how contact would happen.</li></ol><section><h2>If someone disagrees</h2><p><strong>“My favorite rapper dropped out.”</strong> Artists' paths vary. Black Thought's story here includes leaving college. That does not explain what caused success or choose a path for you.</p><p><strong>“I do not need college to make music.”</strong> A college credential is not a universal requirement. What learning and support fit your goal?</p><p><strong>“I am already good with words.”</strong> Start with that strength. Which part would you like more control over?</p><p><strong>“School has not helped me.”</strong> What has been missing or unhelpful? A practical response may matter more than another activity.</p><p><strong>“I need money now.”</strong> Let's find a person who can help with immediate options and a plan that fits.</p><p><strong>“You want me to give up music.”</strong> You choose the music goal. This resource offers practice, learning choices and help.</p></section><section><h2>Safeguarding and evaluation</h2><p>No improvement in attendance, graduation or justice outcomes has been established for this resource.</p><p>Before a pilot with minors, a qualified partner must establish appropriate permission, assent/consent, safeguarding, review, accessibility and data handling. Do not recruit or record minors without those safeguards. Participation must not affect grades, privileges, legal treatment or services. Never request charges, case histories, trauma or autobiographical lyrics.</p></section><a class="button secondary" href="print.html">Open the printable packet</a> <a class="text-link" href="facilitator-outline.pdf" download>Save the one-page facilitator outline</a>'''
sources='''<h1>Sources</h1><section><h2>The six stories</h2><ul class="source-list"><li><a href="https://www.theparisreview.org/blog/2021/04/29/everything-writes-itself-an-interview-with-black-thought/">The Paris Review: Everything Writes Itself</a><p>David Ma · April 29, 2021 · original artist interview.</p></li><li><a href="https://blog.sonicbids.com/rapsody-interview">Sonicbids: An Interview With Rapsody</a><p>Drea · January 8, 2014 · original telephone interview.</p></li><li><a href="https://people.com/music/megan-thee-stallion-pursue-degree-make-late-mom-proud/">PEOPLE: Megan Thee Stallion on continuing school</a><p>Darlene Aderoju · updated June 28, 2020 · original reporting and interview. This is a historical account of studies, not a graduation record.</p></li><li><a href="https://medium.com/cuepoint/gza-speaks-the-lost-art-of-lyricism-ee84510e7268">Cuepoint: GZA Speaks</a><p>Published under GZA’s name · May 27, 2015 · first-person article.</p></li><li><a href="https://daily.redbullmusicacademy.com/2013/02/when-erykah-met-dilla/">Red Bull Music Academy Daily: When Erykah Met Dilla</a><p>Erykah Badu · February 21, 2013 · retrospective first-person lesson account.</p></li><li><a href="https://songexploder.net/little-simz">Song Exploder: Little Simz, episode 294</a><p>2025 artist interview; <a href="https://songexploder.net/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/Song-Exploder-Little-Simz-Transcript.pdf">official transcript</a>, pp. 1 and 8–9.</p></li></ul><p>Stories are paraphrases. Exercises and examples are original teaching material, not artist methods. External sources may contain mature language, ads and tracking.</p></section><section><h2>Musicians’ education and training</h2><p>The <a href="https://www.bls.gov/ooh/entertainment-and-sports/musicians-and-singers.htm">US Bureau of Labor Statistics</a> says musicians and singers typically do not need postsecondary education, while emphasizing training and regular practice. This broad occupation description does not tell a minor to leave secondary school.</p></section><section><h2>Sources for the reference activity</h2><p>Our offline cards paraphrase <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/moon/moonlight/">NASA’s Moonlight</a> (updated February 12, 2026) and the “Clouds and surface” section of <a href="https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Exoplanets/What_do_exoplanets_look_like">ESA’s What do exoplanets look like?</a> (no date displayed; checked October 2, 2026). Both support the narrow reflected-sunlight idea.</p></section><p>Artists and organizations have not endorsed this project.</p>'''
